            <ArticleTitle>Double-blind study of effects of enkephalinase inhibitor on adverse reactions to myelography.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>653-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The side effects in myelography are well known and frequently observed. The most common are headache, nausea, and vomiting. In this study, a rather new compound, Thiorphan, was examined, which displays an antinociceptive activity by inhibiting enkephalinase activity. Forty-two patients received intravenous infusions of Thiorphan before myelography. Another 42 patients were in a control group, and Thiorphan was not administered. In the treated group, postmyelographic headache was found in 24% (versus 52% in the control group). Nausea and vomiting were never seen. Low back pain or sciatica was diminished in 33% of cases. Enkephalin levels in cerebrospinal fluid were measured by a radioreceptor-assay method in both groups without any correlation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
